00:00:21.156 --> 00:00:24.890
PROFESSOR: So a lot of folks,
for instance, the rise in

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.420
online poker or something.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:29.400
And they will say that, oh,
we don't fall into the tax

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.210
bracket for gambling because
we're a skill-based game.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.340
But literally [INAUDIBLE]

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.360
is about competition.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.190
In some ways, you can
class it as sport.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:46.480
But the reason why I was asking
entirely is because the

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.540
way how Caillois writes it, or
at least the way how Roger

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.820
Caillois has been translated,
kind of makes it sound like

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.640
he's actually drawing hard
divisions between all these

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:57.180
categories.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.750
But really they're
all adjectives.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.350
AUDIENCE: What was the
original language?

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:02.311
PROFESSOR: French, I believe.

00:01:02.311 --> 00:01:05.137
AUDIENCE: I believe that's
the original version.

00:01:05.137 --> 00:01:07.963
PROFESSOR: Correct
me if I'm wrong.

00:01:07.963 --> 00:01:09.213
[INTERPOSING  VOICES]

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.600
PROFESSOR: If anyone
can confirm that,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.565
that would be awesome.

00:01:16.565 --> 00:01:17.768
Write a blog post about it.

00:01:17.768 --> 00:01:18.724
AUDIENCE: Jeremy,
you're on it.

00:01:18.724 --> 00:01:20.420
JEREMY: Yeah, I'll try
and confirm that

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:21.060
it's actually French.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.364
I'll confirm it, and then write
an obnoxious post about

00:01:23.364 --> 00:01:24.840
translation errors.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.610
[INTERPOSING  VOICES]

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.440
PROFESSOR: I'm sure it could
have been translated in far

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.256
more interesting language,
because it's kind of dull.

00:01:32.256 --> 00:01:33.190
JEREMY: [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:34.840
French [INAUDIBLE] if it
actually it is French.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.020
PROFESSOR: And then we
have more meetings

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.550
on Caillois on Wednesday.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:38.930
Yay!

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.020
But it's an important text.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:45.140
But [? Ellings ?]

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.910
will be, I use the word vertigo
a lot, because it

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:51.356
sometimes gets sort
of substituted.

00:01:51.356 --> 00:01:55.725
But vertigo, I think people
have a very specific

00:01:55.725 --> 00:02:00.730
understanding of vertigo
as getting

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.500
dizzy because of heights.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:07.270
And obviously not all games
are about heights.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.840
But people, sure, they use it
to describe the sensation of

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.390
taking a roller coaster, which
literally is about heights.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:14.770
But it's also about speed.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.810
And you can use the
same thing.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.956
Have everyone played the game
called Descent on the PC back

00:02:20.956 --> 00:02:22.520
in the day?

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.145
So imagine a first-person
shooter.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.510
The game is filling up
the entire screen.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:33.550
But there is no up.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.000
There's no gravity.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.120
Whenever you want, you can
turn yourself this way.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.390
And you just stay that way
until you want to turn

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:41.700
yourself again.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.490
By the way, the controls work
just pretty much the same way

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:47.370
as a first-person shooter.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:48.915
That was Descent.

00:02:48.915 --> 00:02:53.650
And the whole pleasure of that
game, you'll be doing this all

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:54.066
the friggin' time.

00:02:54.066 --> 00:02:56.310
I'm going down this
straight corridor.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.110
And I never just go down
the straight corridor.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.556
I go down the straight
corridor, and I turn

00:02:59.556 --> 00:03:01.966
[INAUDIBLE].

00:03:01.966 --> 00:03:02.930
AUDIENCE: Inception.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:04.376
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:04.376 --> 00:03:05.822
PROFESSOR: Yeah!

00:03:05.822 --> 00:03:07.268
That sort of thing.

00:03:07.268 --> 00:03:10.530
If you ever get a chance to play
the game Prey, which is

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.640
probably like five dogs on like
a pre-owned nowadays.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:15.240
You have that same
sort of thing.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:20.230
And Portal has a lot of that
kind of loss of control,

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:21.410
physical sensation.

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:22.390
They're trying to get that.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:26.750
But obviously real physical
experiences

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:28.690
that are half an inch.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:34.410
They're talking about
three-legged races or that

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.150
horrible game where you
spin around on a

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.806
bat with your head.

00:03:37.806 --> 00:03:45.050
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:46.790
PROFESSOR: And [? Ellings ?]
can be used to describe any

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:49.410
one of those things.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:52.070
I like to use it as describing
the sort of fun

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.580
of the loss of control.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.000
But that's not necessarily
the case.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.535
Just the sheer physicality of
the game that's part of the

00:03:59.535 --> 00:04:03.857
experience in a way that digital
chess doesn't really

00:04:03.857 --> 00:04:05.444
have any of that.

00:04:05.444 --> 00:04:06.845
Even real chess [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:13.010
So actually I'm just going to
write out those words for

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:16.554
those folks who didn't
get a chance to read

00:04:16.554 --> 00:04:18.399
through that page.

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:19.470
So [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.242
is kind of like intercompetition
skill.

00:04:26.409 --> 00:04:29.216
I talked about [? Ellings ?]
which is kind of jumping down

00:04:29.216 --> 00:04:31.656
through the last one of
these such as-- am I

00:04:31.656 --> 00:04:34.190
spelling this right--

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:37.643
where just kind of physicality
just kind of sucks.

00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:40.998
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:40.998 --> 00:04:42.770
PROFESSOR: Actually Jason,
can you turn the

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:43.722
light in the room on.

00:04:43.722 --> 00:04:44.972
Its extremely dark.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:49.957
Loss of control.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:58.580
Other ones were--

00:04:58.580 --> 00:04:59.610
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:02.650
PROFESSOR: Javier, what
was that again?

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:03.144
AUDIENCE: Chance.

00:05:03.144 --> 00:05:07.096
PROFESSOR: Chance, luck,
not skilled.

00:05:09.943 --> 00:05:15.110
You guys know the phrase
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:16.950
AUDIENCE: The die
had been cast.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:17.480
PROFESSOR: Yes.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.730
Yes, the die has been cast
right before Caesar

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:21.143
[INAUDIBLE]

00:05:21.143 --> 00:05:23.969
and says I now am able
to attack someone.

00:05:23.969 --> 00:05:26.800
I couldn't remember who.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:32.970
But Javier needs
dice basically.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:33.300
PROFESSOR: Actually--

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:34.590
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:35.295
PROFESSOR: Say what?

00:05:35.295 --> 00:05:36.670
AUDIENCE: He matched on
the [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:37.380
PROFESSOR: Matched?

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:38.250
AUDIENCE: Matched on
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:39.500
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:45.846
Although I've heard two
translations of The Die Has

00:05:45.846 --> 00:05:47.560
Been Cast as a phrase
actually.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.345
Because on one hand, you
can think of it as the

00:05:50.345 --> 00:05:50.980
die has been cast.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:55.800
So we'll see where fortune takes
us, and there's nothing

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.350
we can do wrong.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:04.260
But I've also heard it described
as the die as in the

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:10.600
thing that you put molten
metal into, as in cast.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:14.695
And so whatever we were
making is now set.

00:06:17.702 --> 00:06:22.790
And they had that same idea, but
because we had because one

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:26.920
way that you can think of is
this is not auto control.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:29.070
And the other way you can think
of this is and now this

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:30.170
is set in stone.

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:33.370
Which are two very different
connotations, but both saying

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:35.670
we don't really have control
of the situation.

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:37.030
AUDIENCE: Yassa means thrown.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:38.325
PROFESSOR: Thrown away, yeah.

00:06:38.325 --> 00:06:38.750
Yeah.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:42.780
But the English phrase,
[INAUDIBLE].

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:46.290
AUDIENCE: Are there words in
English that [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.400
root to mean random?

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:51.820
In French the word for
random is aleatoire.

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:52.780
Come straight from
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:53.320
PROFESSOR: Sure.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:54.390
Yeah.

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:56.175
PROFESSOR: Which is undoubtably
why he chose that

00:06:56.175 --> 00:06:57.710
word I just checked,
he is French.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:04.925
PROFESSOR: Obviously that's
why Caillois used it.

00:07:04.925 --> 00:07:09.340
But yeah, I can't think of any
English words that use that as

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:13.672
a root, although there
might be some.

00:07:13.672 --> 00:07:15.320
I know there's a
game committee.

00:07:18.206 --> 00:07:19.649
And I think they actually
make strategic games.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:25.922
And there's one more
which is--

00:07:25.922 --> 00:07:27.172
AUDIENCE: Mimicry.

00:07:32.188 --> 00:07:35.160
PROFESSOR: Which is kind of odd
because that's in English,

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:36.410
not in French.

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:41.750
Mimicry's mimicry.

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:45.200
It's actually in English,
so role playing,

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:48.790
dress up, let's pretend.

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.045
Simulations sometimes?

00:07:51.045 --> 00:07:52.870
You know, it might actually
fall into that.

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:58.660
So you can imagine in tactical
simulation, for instance,

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:02.530
being very much about
competition and skills.

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:08.950
But then if it's a tactical
simulation about Russian

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:11.360
[UNINTELLIGIBLE], something
like that, then

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:12.610
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:18.600
territory.

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:18.875
Do you--

00:08:18.875 --> 00:08:21.195
It's part of the pleasure of
believing that you are

00:08:21.195 --> 00:08:24.200
something else, as opposed
to I'm just guy playing

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:25.280
[INAUDIBLE]

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:31.208
as opposed to I am this 17th
century scholar [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:31.208 --> 00:08:35.100
And making a game about that.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:40.340
So these are the adjectives
that he talks about to

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:43.640
describe games, but in the bit
where we actually ask you to

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:47.750
read, it's more about why.

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:51.600
These words give you a clue to
think about why are people

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:53.852
playing games.

00:08:53.852 --> 00:08:58.393
These are types of fun, much in
a way that gets touched on

00:08:58.393 --> 00:09:03.820
by the MDA paper that we
had you read last week.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:06.090
What are these aesthetics that
people are going for?

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:09.892
Are they going for the thrill
of competition or

00:09:09.892 --> 00:09:11.360
the thrill of luck?

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:14.901
Are they going for physical
motion and how that feels, or

00:09:14.901 --> 00:09:18.510
are they going for the feeling
of being someone else?

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:20.330
And it's not--

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:22.980
Again, these are not mutually
exclusive terms.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:25.220
These are things that you could
have one game that has

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:26.025
all of this.

00:09:26.025 --> 00:09:29.400
You could have one game that
has two in some strong

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:33.136
competition and the rest
are all minimized.

00:09:33.136 --> 00:09:33.905
That's fine.

00:09:33.905 --> 00:09:36.977
But understanding that this is
why people are enjoying the

00:09:36.977 --> 00:09:39.410
game more, and more importantly
this is what

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:43.950
people are enjoying playing
with each other for.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:46.770
One nice thing about this
is that fodder for

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:47.580
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:51.250
and for mimicry, it's really,
really difficult to think

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:53.960
about those two in particular
without

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:55.880
thinking about the players.

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:58.998
Competition sucks if
there's no one else

00:09:58.998 --> 00:10:00.950
you're competing with.

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:05.342
Sure playing against computers
nowadays, but

00:10:05.342 --> 00:10:08.270
traditionally we couldn't.

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:11.198
At least you were playing
against the designer, and

00:10:11.198 --> 00:10:13.590
especially when it
came puzzles.

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:16.728
It's really, really annoying to
pretend to be somebody else

00:10:16.728 --> 00:10:19.584
when there's no audience, even
if that other person is

00:10:19.584 --> 00:10:21.488
someone pretending
along with you.

00:10:21.488 --> 00:10:23.392
[INAUDIBLE].

00:10:23.392 --> 00:10:24.670
Now, luck--

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:29.050
you can continue to play
a game flipping a coin.

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:32.080
But even when it comes to
luck, and it comes to

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:36.650
experiences of physical motion,
these things tend to

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:37.885
be measurable [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:37.885 --> 00:10:42.355
And that's why these four
particular divisions that

00:10:42.355 --> 00:10:43.285
[INAUDIBLE]

00:10:43.285 --> 00:10:47.005
picked out are really, really
good for [INAUDIBLE] social

00:10:47.005 --> 00:10:47.935
[INAUDIBLE].

00:10:47.935 --> 00:10:52.280
A game where is there a name for
this game where you put a

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:55.745
bat on the ground and
you go around?

00:10:55.745 --> 00:10:57.230
AUDIENCE: Bat?

00:10:57.230 --> 00:10:59.705
AUDIENCE: Bat irritating.

00:10:59.705 --> 00:11:02.680
AUDIENCE: What is he
talking about?

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:05.540
AUDIENCE: Wait, wait, and
someone gets fixed and then?

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:09.033
PROFESSOR: So there's a type of
relay race where you pick

00:11:09.033 --> 00:11:11.175
up a baseball bat, and you
put your head on it.

00:11:11.175 --> 00:11:14.870
You go around it three times,
and then you just run back to

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:19.504
your team, carry the bat, and
try to hand the bat off to

00:11:19.504 --> 00:11:20.250
somebody else on your team.

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:23.130
Usually ending up
hitting someone.

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:24.710
AUDIENCE: I've heard it
called Dizzy Bats.

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:25.090
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:25.450
AUDIENCE: Dizzy Bats.

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:25.740
AUDIENCE: Dizzy Bats.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:26.880
PROFESSOR: Dizzy Bats.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:27.090
Yeah.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:31.650
So that game is a lot more
fun when there's

00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:33.130
somebody else around.

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:35.455
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:35.455 --> 00:11:38.710
AUDIENCE: I do that by myself.

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:42.000
AUDIENCE: You start handing
it off to yourself.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.172
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:44.172 --> 00:11:46.850
AUDIENCE: Bats flying around.

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:50.930
PROFESSOR: And even with games
of chance, I mean sure, if you

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:54.900
cook the games of chance by
itself, but for the most part

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:58.066
most of my enjoyment of the game
of chance is usually with

00:11:58.066 --> 00:12:01.400
a group of four people,
actually, either playing poker

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:04.400
or playing something
like mahjong.

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:07.000
So these are all very
support [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:09.900
If you think about when
someone's going to come down

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:15.645
and sit in front of your game,
and what sort of frame of mind

00:12:15.645 --> 00:12:20.409
they're going to be in
to play your game?

00:12:20.409 --> 00:12:23.109
What sort of frame of mind do
you want to put them in in

00:12:23.109 --> 00:12:24.130
order to enjoy the games?

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:27.136
Think about why they
might want to hang

00:12:27.136 --> 00:12:28.369
out with other people.

00:12:28.369 --> 00:12:29.619
Because [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:32.313 --> 00:12:35.437
So if there's this other person
they play with, that

00:12:35.437 --> 00:12:39.150
you may or may not know, but try
to think about how you're

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:40.530
going to construct enough
clues to sort of get

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.112
them to enjoy it.

00:12:42.112 --> 00:12:46.310
So the game is about pretending
to be someone else.

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:50.280
I'm going to make a game where I
am playing a negotiator in a

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:52.780
hostage situation,
for instance.

00:12:52.780 --> 00:12:54.780
[INAUDIBLE], awesome.

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:55.968
I'm going to be the
negotiator.

00:12:55.968 --> 00:13:00.380
You all are going to be the
guy taking hostages.

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:03.800
And then you want to play
that out, and harm them.

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:08.240
Friday we had the game Crunch,
where people were playing

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:11.570
CEOs, I think.

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:12.840
I'm hoping [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:14.813
that you will already
hate [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:14.813 --> 00:13:16.005
AUDIENCE: That's awesome.

00:13:16.005 --> 00:13:16.820
PROFESSOR: [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:17.840
less corrupt.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:21.086
And you think about how does the
game, even from the rules,

00:13:21.086 --> 00:13:24.167
even from the design of the box,
get you into the mood of

00:13:24.167 --> 00:13:30.837
thinking, OK, whoever I am in
real life, I'm going to take

00:13:30.837 --> 00:13:31.514
on this persona.

00:13:31.514 --> 00:13:33.550
And I'm going to enjoy taking
on this persona for a while.

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:35.320
AUDIENCE: Yeah, but then you're
really not [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:37.382
because they have this game's
kind of built in where you

00:13:37.382 --> 00:13:40.455
actually hide cards
on yourself.

00:13:40.455 --> 00:13:42.880
AUDIENCE: Did you guys catch
anyone doing that in the act?

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:43.850
AUDIENCE: I got caught.

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:46.480
AUDIENCE: No, I caught him.

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:47.550
AUDIENCE: I feel like
arguing about

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:49.580
that's part of the rules.

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:50.920
He already got it.

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:53.455
He had already hidden them away,
and then the cards were

00:13:53.455 --> 00:13:56.830
sought way afterwards.

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:59.980
PROFESSOR: And even arguing
over whether something was

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:02.670
illegal or not, even when
there's evidence that it is,

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:05.316
no one caught you doing it.

00:14:05.316 --> 00:14:08.836
It's appropriate for this theme
of being a CEO of a

00:14:08.836 --> 00:14:10.570
failing bank.

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:16.080
It's like, well, so we have
all of these CDOs.

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:18.180
Yeah, we purchased them at some
point, but nobody caught

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:19.270
us while we were doing it.

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:22.370
So now you guys have
to help us fix it.

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:23.850
That's what the game's about.

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:30.270
Was there a game that was
particularly competitive that

00:14:30.270 --> 00:14:32.300
people played on Friday?

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:34.635
AUDIENCE: That game
was pretty good.

00:14:34.635 --> 00:14:35.330
PROFESSOR: Which one?

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:36.272
[INAUDIBLE].

00:14:36.272 --> 00:14:39.710
AUDIENCE: There was a
competitive moment in Saboteur

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:45.330
when Alec, revealed himself to
be a saboteur and ruined our

00:14:45.330 --> 00:14:48.010
chance to find the gold.

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:52.770
PROFESSOR: Saboteur is what I
would describe as cutthroat.

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:57.610
It's a game that's deliberately
about winning by

00:14:57.610 --> 00:15:00.120
other people losing.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:02.702
AUDIENCE: It made me
upset with Alec.

00:15:02.702 --> 00:15:04.520
AUDIENCE: It made me cry.

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:08.127
PROFESSOR: And John Nash,
actually, [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:21.330
Oh.

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:25.320
Because he did spend some time
here at MIT, possibly when

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:30.380
visiting, but he designed one
game called Hex, which is

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:36.345
ostensibly designed based on the
washroom tiles of one of

00:15:36.345 --> 00:15:42.300
the Building 39 or Building
37 bathrooms.

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:47.070
But anyway, that game and
another game called Screw Your

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:50.330
Buddies are all cutthroat
games.

00:15:50.330 --> 00:15:53.700
The only way to get them to win
this game is by screwing

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:57.730
over the other person playing.

00:15:57.730 --> 00:16:00.430
Screw Your Buddies in particular
is just like boiled

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:02.702
down diplomacy.

00:16:02.702 --> 00:16:04.892
The only way you're going to
win this game is to betray

00:16:04.892 --> 00:16:05.520
someone else.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:07.550
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:09.026
PROFESSOR: Let's see.

00:16:09.026 --> 00:16:09.938
Games of chance.

00:16:09.938 --> 00:16:12.510
What was kind of chancy
on Friday?

00:16:12.510 --> 00:16:13.120
And you--

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:14.370
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:18.045
PROFESSOR: Think or go?

00:16:18.045 --> 00:16:18.400
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:21.200
AUDIENCE: That's 17.

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:23.010
[INAUDIBLE]

00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:24.710
4:00 AM you've got--

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:26.045
AUDIENCE: Every threat
was on the board.

00:16:26.045 --> 00:16:27.520
Two people go in.

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:28.550
17 drops.

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:30.400
Oh my God.

00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:33.260
PROFESSOR: It's a
press-your-luck game, right?

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:34.550
Do you dare?

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:36.026
Do you think you can?

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:43.410
I enjoy that game a lot.

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:44.660
[INAUDIBLE].

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:49.890
I don't think that we had any
influence specifically.

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:54.550
It's hard to get really physical
in card games.

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:57.250
AUDIENCE: Maybe in that
that game, Falling.

00:16:57.250 --> 00:16:57.505
Yeah.

00:16:57.505 --> 00:16:59.940
That was a pit.

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:02.580
PROFESSOR: Pit actually, you're
right, two weeks ago,

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:04.295
some of the games that
were played were

00:17:04.295 --> 00:17:06.644
really kind of kinetic.

00:17:06.644 --> 00:17:10.684
So even with card games, you
already have very good

00:17:10.684 --> 00:17:13.160
examples of that.

00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:17.962
I want you to keep in mind
because today we're actually

00:17:17.962 --> 00:17:20.940
going to start the process of
getting new folks to think

00:17:20.940 --> 00:17:23.687
about what you're going to do as
social assignment and break

00:17:23.687 --> 00:17:26.270
you up into teams.

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:31.585
Before we continue , Jason, do
you have anything to raise

00:17:31.585 --> 00:17:33.070
regarding [INAUDIBLE]?

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:34.920
No.

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:36.660
Anybody else?

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:39.370
AUDIENCE: I'm still confused
about the whole Eskimo part.

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:41.770
AUDIENCE: Agreed.

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:43.020
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:48.950
AUDIENCE: Eskimos.

00:17:48.950 --> 00:17:50.310
PROFESSOR: Take a look
at the French.

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:51.390
[INAUDIBLE]

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:52.710
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:54.590
and what took universities,
some are a good

00:17:54.590 --> 00:17:55.300
friend of ours did.

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:57.990
I mean we might know someone
who knows him.

00:17:57.990 --> 00:17:59.980
I'll find out tonight.

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:00.500
[INAUDIBLE]

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:01.720
is not that old.

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:03.160
So maybe--

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:05.390
AUDIENCE: We to just have him
come over over, chill at MIT

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:07.365
for a little bit.

00:18:07.365 --> 00:18:09.300
We could talk to him
about the Eskimos.

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:10.550
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:19.431
AUDIENCE: He is dead, but
I might know some

00:18:19.431 --> 00:18:21.355
people who knew him.

00:18:21.355 --> 00:18:23.700
Just be a necromancer,
that's OK.

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:24.210
PROFESSOR: All right.

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:27.910
So this is what we're
going to do.

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:30.630
We're basically going to
do brainstorming today.

00:18:30.630 --> 00:18:33.662
The basic idea is that we're
going to have a bunch of index

00:18:33.662 --> 00:18:39.670
cards and one job to make it
easier on the job of the

00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:43.180
secretary is that you'd actually
be reading off

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:44.390
something written.

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:46.910
So especially while other people
are throwing out their

00:18:46.910 --> 00:18:49.250
ideas, and you get something,
and that

00:18:49.250 --> 00:18:50.030
person's still talking.

00:18:50.030 --> 00:18:52.630
You might as well just
write down your idea.

00:18:52.630 --> 00:18:54.470
So we'll have a whole
bunch of cards.

00:18:58.340 --> 00:19:01.280
I'd also like you to start
off by writing your

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:03.280
name right on top.

00:19:03.280 --> 00:19:06.280
And the reason for this is
because I don't think every

00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:10.410
single person in this room knows
each other's names yet.

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:13.835
As we do this process, it's
more that you remember oh

00:19:13.835 --> 00:19:17.080
yeah, it's that guy who came
up with that idea.

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:19.773
Oh, and that person's
name is so-and-so.

00:19:23.286 --> 00:19:24.708
AUDIENCE: That's
what you want?

00:19:24.708 --> 00:19:27.236
To have all the ideas from each
person on one card, or

00:19:27.236 --> 00:19:29.190
have one idea per card?

00:19:29.190 --> 00:19:30.100
PROFESSOR: All on one.

00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:31.930
AUDIENCE: What if the professors
took notes and we

00:19:31.930 --> 00:19:33.620
could just bounce out ideas?

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:35.920
PROFESSOR: Well that's what
the tape recorder is for.

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:38.330
But the thing about writing
your ideas down is that

00:19:38.330 --> 00:19:41.600
someone can look at the card
and say, yeah, that guy.

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:43.150
I want to follow up
with this guy.

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:47.820
Because we're not actually going
to form the teams for

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:49.430
you by the end of this class.

00:19:49.430 --> 00:19:56.360
The idea is so that after this
brainstorm, you've got a pool

00:19:56.360 --> 00:20:00.620
of ideas of which you
guys can make games.

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:04.875
Actually, frankly, we don't
care if two teams ended up

00:20:04.875 --> 00:20:07.947
making pretty much the same
game, out of the same concept.

00:20:07.947 --> 00:20:09.903
Because I know that two
different teams of two

00:20:09.903 --> 00:20:13.000
different sets of people are
going end up making different

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:16.750
games anyway, even if you
start from the same.

00:20:16.750 --> 00:20:18.730
That's not a requirement.

00:20:18.730 --> 00:20:21.700
You folks can make--

00:20:21.700 --> 00:20:25.840
you can take any set of ideas
from the book today.

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:29.950
Obviously, if you're the one who
pitch an idea and somebody

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:32.750
else thinks, hey, that was
actually pretty cool.

00:20:32.750 --> 00:20:35.215
Take that opportunity to
talk to the person and

00:20:35.215 --> 00:20:37.790
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:41.010
That pretty much is going to
happen between classes.

00:20:41.010 --> 00:20:44.660
So by Wednesday, I want
you all to come in.

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:47.198
This is basically one of the
ideas of the homework, is to

00:20:47.198 --> 00:20:51.460
tell me which team you're on,
who's on your team, and what's

00:20:51.460 --> 00:20:56.210
the general one line idea that
you're working with.

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:57.960
Now, the idea that they're
giving us is

00:20:57.960 --> 00:20:58.980
what you started with.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:00.630
It may not be what
you end with.

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:01.900
That's fine.

00:21:01.900 --> 00:21:04.560
I'm very comfortable
that ideas change

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:05.440
while you guys work.

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:06.400
I think that's fine.

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:07.800
The teams shouldn't change.

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:11.140
So you're working with these
three other people,

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:12.160
or a team of a four.

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:17.398
Then that should be intact
for the rest of this--

00:21:17.398 --> 00:21:18.880
for this assignment.

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:25.580
OK, so teams of four.

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:33.565
Now, one more clarification
about the project.

00:21:33.565 --> 00:21:39.460
The card game, all the cards
need to be identically sized.

00:21:39.460 --> 00:21:42.940
We do like cards that are going
to be legible, but you

00:21:42.940 --> 00:21:43.920
don't need to spend
a whole lot of

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:45.350
time making them pretty.

00:21:45.350 --> 00:21:49.230
I will just appreciate it that
by the time you actually hand

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:53.760
in your cards, you have
them written in ink.

00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:55.952
Pencil is great when you're
prototyping because you can

00:21:55.952 --> 00:21:57.810
erase [UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:21:57.810 --> 00:21:59.670
Ink is great for creating the
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:09.410
Your game can be playable by
two, three, or four players.

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:14.760
It does not need to cater to any
grouping other than what

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:15.385
you decide.

00:22:15.385 --> 00:22:18.352
So if you're saying, this is a
two-player game, we will test

00:22:18.352 --> 00:22:19.100
it with two people.

00:22:19.100 --> 00:22:22.260
If you're saying this is two
to four, we'll test it with

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:24.420
some number with however many
people we manage to

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:25.660
rustle up to test.

00:22:25.660 --> 00:22:28.160
If you say it could be
two or four, but not

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:29.392
three, that's fine.

00:22:29.392 --> 00:22:31.865
You think I kid, but there are
some games that work really,

00:22:31.865 --> 00:22:32.910
really well with even numbers.

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:34.160
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:22:38.270 --> 00:22:40.920
PROFESSOR: Keep it
four and under.

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:45.540
Or at least make it possible
for us to grade it with a

00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:46.650
maximum of four people.

00:22:46.650 --> 00:22:49.780
Because if you give us a game
that requires five people, we

00:22:49.780 --> 00:22:52.335
have to find five people and get
them together in order to

00:22:52.335 --> 00:22:53.585
create your [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:01.420
I think there was one more
requirement for how long the

00:23:01.420 --> 00:23:02.250
game could take.

00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:03.270
AUDIENCE: I think it's
5 to 10 minutes.

00:23:03.270 --> 00:23:04.040
AUDIENCE: It was short.

00:23:04.040 --> 00:23:05.040
AUDIENCE: That's really short.

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:06.110
AUDIENCE: That's super short.

00:23:06.110 --> 00:23:08.895
AUDIENCE: Falling
is really short.

00:23:08.895 --> 00:23:09.440
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:11.025
AUDIENCE: Crunch is not.

00:23:11.025 --> 00:23:12.275
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.370
AUDIENCE: So when you're
brainstorming for game ideas,

00:23:16.370 --> 00:23:18.337
how much do we
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE] come

00:23:18.337 --> 00:23:21.240
to up with in our brainstorm?

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:22.200
It's a cool idea.

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:24.536
It's an amusing space.

00:23:24.536 --> 00:23:27.260
PROFESSOR: I will say that if
the character's the thing

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:29.860
that's encouraging you to
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:23:32.740 --> 00:23:36.210
I have this cool game
that comes to mind.

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:40.855
Then that's the thing that
you're throwing out.

00:23:40.855 --> 00:23:44.430
If instead, I want to make
a game about fish.

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:46.680
I have no game mechanics
in mind, but I

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:47.928
think fish are awesome.

00:23:47.928 --> 00:23:49.302
Then, OK.

00:23:49.302 --> 00:23:51.140
That's the game you're
doing now.

00:23:51.140 --> 00:23:56.010
Maybe that's the first bad idea
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:23:56.010 --> 00:23:59.230
AUDIENCE: If we have like
several game ideas, should we

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:02.500
take several index cards
and write one per?

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:05.210
PROFESSOR: Actually, no I would
rather see them all on

00:24:05.210 --> 00:24:06.790
one if you can fit them.

00:24:06.790 --> 00:24:09.210
And if you run out of space,
flip them over.

00:24:09.210 --> 00:24:12.950
Again, the idea of the index
cards is not just to record

00:24:12.950 --> 00:24:16.775
the ideas down, because what I
am going to do is I'm going to

00:24:16.775 --> 00:24:19.250
try to type them all out,
so that you can

00:24:19.250 --> 00:24:20.500
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:24:22.940 --> 00:24:25.740
But also to find you
folks again.

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:27.280
So I said, yeah that was
that guy who had

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:28.250
this idea about fish.

00:24:28.250 --> 00:24:29.500
Who was that again?

00:24:34.555 --> 00:24:39.170
So I guess I will leave you.

00:24:39.170 --> 00:24:40.397
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:24:40.397 --> 00:24:41.888
AUDIENCE: Want me to
write them down?

00:24:41.888 --> 00:24:43.379
PROFESSOR: I think that
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:24:46.860 --> 00:24:49.360
AUDIENCE: I mean, you're
not a facilitator?

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:50.656
PROFESSOR: Yeah, I'm
facilitating.

00:24:50.656 --> 00:24:54.400
AUDIENCE: I like clerk better.

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:55.650
AUDIENCE:
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:25:02.730 --> 00:25:03.980
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:25:10.003 --> 00:25:11.422
PROFESSOR: But I am actually
wonder whether we should

00:25:11.422 --> 00:25:14.400
define this into two groups.

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:15.780
AUDIENCE: We get to
cross-pollinate.

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:16.740
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:20.880
PROFESSOR: Let's work as
a whole class first.

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:23.290
I know there were some folks who
actually started off with

00:25:23.290 --> 00:25:25.005
ideas already on the forum.

00:25:25.005 --> 00:25:28.585
So why don't those folks throw
your ideas first because you

00:25:28.585 --> 00:25:29.845
already mentioned
in the forum.

00:25:29.845 --> 00:25:32.850
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I wasn't part
of that group in the forum.

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:35.250
AUDIENCE: Well, to start off,
Jeremy and I have been sort of

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:37.290
discussing the idea, you
want to go ahead?

